<spell-notebook "audio usage">
	<header level="1">
		Audio
		<header>An audio plays a sound file:  in the browser's own player, or unseen, when the page says.</>
	</>
	<audio-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-audio>` plays a sound file:  in the browser's own player with `controls`, or unseen.

			- `src` is the file, relative to the page.
			- With `controls`, the browser's player shows.  Without, nothing does:  the page plays it with `play()`.
			- `autoplay` starts it at once, but a browser only allows that when it's `muted`, or after the reader has clicked
			  on the page.
			- `muted` and `loop` are the player's own.

			From script:  `audio.play()`, `audio.pause()`, `audio.currentTime`, `audio.paused`, and the events `ui-play`,
			`ui-pause` and `ui-ended`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- Name a player with `label`.
			- Don't let a sound play by itself:  people using screen readers hear it over their reader.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
